{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgatnt4ES3pAV6fNCTCRa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Short answer:** I know of no published result for $V_f(x,y)$ itself, and I won't invent one. What follows is what I recall about neighbouring results, with confidence levels. I have no tools here, so none of it is checked.\n\n## 1. Results for $V_f(x,y)$ or very close variants\n\n- I know of no paper, thesis or preprint that treats the polynomial-weighted sparse variance $\\sum_{k\\le y} f'(k)\\sum_{l\\le f(k)}E(x;f(k),l)^2$. If one exists (for example a recent arXiv preprint), I don't know it.\n- **Heuristic only, not a theorem.** For a single modulus $q$, I'd expect $\\sum_{l \\bmod q}E(x;q,l)^2 \\asymp x^{1/2}q^{1/2}$ in a suitable range, with an arithmetic constant depending on $q$. The weight $f'(k)$ makes the moduli $f(k)$ behave like a Stieltjes measure $dq$. For $\\deg f=d$, this gives\n  $$V_f(x,y)\\approx C_f\\,x^{1/2}\\sum_{k\\le y} f'(k)f(k)^{1/2}\\sim \\tfrac23 C_f\\,x^{1/2}f(y)^{3/2}.$$\n  Here $C_f$ is not the all-moduli constant. It should be an average of the local factors over $q=f(k)$, so it depends on how $f$ distributes modulo $p^j$.\n- I haven't verified this heuristic, so treat it as a conjecture.\n\n## 2. Related results I recall\n\n**Single modulus and all moduli $q\\le Y$:**\n\n- **Hooley (1970s, Bull. London Math. Soc. note on squarefree numbers in APs).** Upper bounds for $\\sum_{l \\bmod q}E^2$, and I think a conjecture of order $x^{1/2}q^{1/2}$ up to $\\varepsilon$-losses. *Confidence: moderate on the topic and author, low on exact exponents.*\n- **Warlimont (around 1980, J. London Math. Soc., squarefree numbers in APs).** Asymptotic and mean-square results on the same problem. *Confidence: low to moderate on the details.*\n- **Blomer (c. 2008, \"The average value of divisor sums in arithmetic progressions\", Quart. J. Math.).** An asymptotic formula for the variance averaged over $q\\le Q$, with main term of shape $c\\,x^{1/2}Q^{3/2}$. The range of $Q$ is wider than Hooley's. *Confidence: moderate that this result exists and is attributed correctly, low on the precise range and error term.*\n- **Nunes (c. 2016, J. Number Theory, squarefree numbers in APs).** Upper bounds for the second moment over residues, in a range of $q$ up to a power of $x$. *Confidence: moderate on existence, low on the statement.*\n- **Le Boudec (2010s).** Work on the variance and moments of squarefree counts in progressions, including the Hooley-type conjecture. *Confidence: low to moderate.*\n\n**Sparse variance for primes:**\n\n- I don't recall a specific theorem for $\\sum_{p\\le y}\\sum_l E(x;p,l)^2$. Prime moduli are a natural case, since $q=p$ is squarefree and the local factors are simple, but I can't cite a paper. *Confidence that I know of none: moderate.*\n\n## 3. Confidence summary\n\n| Item | Confidence |\n|---|---|\n| No known result for $V_f$ | Moderate. My knowledge could be incomplete or out of date. |\n| Existence of Hooley, Warlimont, Blomer, Nunes, Le Boudec work in this area | Moderate to low. I'm not sure of exact titles, years or journals. |\n| Exponents, constants and ranges for those results | Low. Check the originals. |\n| Heuristic $x^{1/2}f(y)^{3/2}$ | My own extrapolation, not a cited result. |\n| Prime-modulus sparse result | None known to me. |\n\nBefore relying on any of this, check MathSciNet, zbMATH or arXiv, searching \"variance squarefree arithmetic progressions\" and the author names above."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3714,"cache_read_input_tokens":649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3714},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null